rates and observe convergence vs divergence\n- Apply the trained model to real-world mental health screening scenarios\n- Understand ethical considerations for predictive mental health models\n\n## Notation Summary\n\n| Notation | Description | Python Variable |\n|:---------|:------------|:----------------|\n| $m$ | Number of patient observations | `m` |\n| $x^{(i)}$ | Social isolation index for patient $i$ (0-10 scale) | `x_train[i]` |\n| $y^{(i)}$ | Mental health risk score for patient $i$ (0-100 scale) | `y_train[i]` |\n| $w$ | Risk sensitivity per isolation unit (slope) | `w` |\n| $b$ | Baseline risk score at zero isolation (intercept) | `b` |\n| $\\alpha$ | Learning rate (step size) | `alpha` |\n| $\\frac{\\partial J}{\\partial w}$ | Partial derivative of cost w.r.t. $w$ | `dj_dw` |\n| $\\frac{\\partial J}{\\partial b}$ | Partial derivative of cost w.r.t. $b$ | `dj_db` |\n| $J(w,b)$ | Cost function (mean squared error) | `J_history` |"
