      "content": "## Problem Statement\n\n### Clinical Context\n\nResearchers from a community mental health study collected anonymized data from 10 participants. Each participant completed a standardized Social Isolation Index (SII) assessment scoring from 0 (highly connected, strong social network) to 10 (extremely isolated, minimal social contact). Participants also received a composite Mental Health Risk Score (MHRS) from 0 (minimal risk) to 100 (severe risk requiring immediate intervention).\n\nThe SII incorporates factors such as living alone, frequency of social contact, participation in group activities, and perceived loneliness. The MHRS combines clinical assessments, self-reported symptom inventories, and functional impairment ratings.\n\n### Dataset: Community Mental Health Survey\n\n| Participant | SII Score | MHRS Score |\n|:------------|:----------|:------------|\n| 1 | 1.5 | 12.0 |\n| 2 | 2.0 | 18.0 |\n| 3 | 3.0 | 22.0 |\n| 4 | 4.0 | 35.0 |\n| 5 | 5.0 | 40.0 |\n| 6 | 5.5 | 48.0 |\n| 7 | 6.5 | 55.0 |\n| 8 | 7.0 | 62.0 |\n| 9 | 8.5 | 72.0 |\n| 10 | 9.5 | 85.0 |\n\n**Clinical Observation:** A clear positive trend exists: higher social isolation correlates with higher mental health risk. However, the relationship is not perfectly linear due to individual resilience factors, coping mechanisms, and baseline mental health conditions."
