      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "content": "## Learning Rate Analysis: Convergence vs Divergence\n\n### The Goldilocks Problem\n\nThe learning rate determines step size. Three regimes exist:\n\n1. **Too small (alpha=0.001):** Converges correctly but takes 10x longer. In production systems serving real-time predictions, this delay matters.\n\n2. **Just right (alpha=0.01):** Smooth, monotonic convergence. Cost decreases rapidly at first, then gradually approaches minimum.\n\n3. **Too large (alpha=0.1+):** Parameters overshoot the minimum. Cost oscillates and increases. The model diverges, producing increasingly wild predictions.\n\n### Clinical Consequence of Divergence\n\nIf gradient descent diverges on a mental health model, the resulting parameters could predict risk scores ranging from -500 to +1000. A hospital using such a model would flag nearly everyone as high-risk or miss everyone entirely. The cost function's increase during divergence is a critical warning sign that must be monitored in any production ML system."
