      "content": "## Key Takeaways\n\n### Mathematical\n\n1. **Gradient descent automates optimization.** Instead of manually searching parameter space, the algorithm uses calculus to find the steepest descent direction and follows it to the minimum cost.\n\n2. **Simultaneous updates are critical.** Both gradients must be computed before either parameter changes. Sequential updates introduce bias and can prevent convergence.\n\n3. **Learning rate is the most important hyperparameter.** Too small wastes computation; too large causes divergence. Monitoring cost history for monotonic decrease is the simplest convergence diagnostic.\n\n4. **Convergence is asymptotic.** Progress is rapid initially (large gradients far from minimum) and slows near the optimum (small gradients on flat valley floor). This motivates adaptive learning rate methods in advanced courses.\n\n### Mental Health and Ethical\n\n1. **Predictive models can enable early intervention.** Identifying at-risk individuals before crisis episodes can save lives. Gradient descent makes scalable screening possible.\n\n2. **Single-variable models are dangerously limited.** Social isolation alone cannot capture the complexity of mental health risk. Production systems must incorporate multiple assessment dimensions.\n\n3. **False negatives are more harmful than false positives.** Missing a high-risk patient has potentially fatal consequences. The cost function should potentially weight false negatives more heavily (asymmetric loss).\n\n4. **Algorithmic bias is a real threat.** If training data underrepresents certain demographics, the model will perform poorly for those groups, potentially widening health disparities.\n\n5. **Models must complement, not replace, clinicians.** The gradient descent algorithm produces a mathematical optimum, but clinical judgment incorporates context, nuance, and human connection that no model captures.\n\n### What Comes Next\n\nFuture labs extend gradient descent to **multiple features** (adding sleep data, mobility patterns, social media sentiment) using multivariate linear regression, and eventually to **nonlinear models** via neural networks. The core optimization principle remains identical: compute gradients, step opposite, monitor cost."
